[?25l> There it is! The second clamp acc < -0x80000000LL is triggering! On a 64-bit system, -0x80000000LL is -2147483648 as a long long. But acc is size_t (unsigned). The comparison acc < -0x80000000LL — in C, when comparing size_t (unsigned) with long long (signed), the usual arithmetic conversions apply. Since size_t is unsigned long (64-bit) and long long is signed 64-bit, the long long gets converted to unsigned long long. So -0x80000000LL becomes 0xFFFFFFFF80000000ULL = 18446744071562067968. And 9 < 18446744071562067968 is TRUE! So acc gets set to (size_t)-0x80000000LL = 18446744071562067968, and (int)acc = -2147483648.

This is a C bug — the comparison is nonsensical for unsigned types, but we must reproduce it. The fix in Rust: acc is usize, and we need to compare it against -0x80000000i64 converted to usize the same way C does.
